msgid "IDS_ADECK_LEVEL_1_TEXT"
msgstr ""
"The pace of the evacuation was slow in the early days of Operation Dynamo, "
"but soon, every available civilian ship was mobilized, expanding the "
"evacuation fleet to several hundred vessels."

msgid "IDS_ADECK_LEVEL_1_TITLE"
msgstr "Little Ships of Dunkirk"

msgid "IDS_ADECK_LEVEL_2_TEXT"
msgstr ""
"Former river steamers and fishing trawlers sailed alongside destroyers, "
"navigating minefields, enduring harsh weather, and repelling Luftwaffe air "
"raids, as well as attacks from Kriegsmarine motor torpedo boats and "
"submarines. On May 29, 47,000 soldiers were evacuated; on May 31, another "
"68,000 were rescued from the French coast and brought to Britain."

msgid "IDS_ADECK_LEVEL_2_TITLE"
msgstr "In the Eye of the Storm"

msgid "IDS_ADECK_LEVEL_3_TEXT"
msgstr ""
"The pressure intensified with each passing day. Luftwaffe air raids grew "
"deadlier, Wehrmacht forces advanced toward the coast, and German artillery "
"began targeting evacuation ships. Despite heavy losses, 64,000 more people "
"were evacuated on June 1. From the next day onward, the evacuation took "
"place only at night. By June 4, 1940, when Operation Dynamo came to an end, "
"more than 850 warships and civilian vessels—many making multiple trips—had "
"successfully evacuated 338,000 British and French soldiers."

msgid "IDS_ADECK_LEVEL_3_TITLE"
msgstr "The Miracle of Dunkirk"

msgid "IDS_ADECK_MAIN_REWARD_CLAIMED_TEXT"
msgstr ""
"You have successfully completed the evacuation! Here is your final reward."

msgid "IDS_ADECK_MAIN_REWARD_CLAIMED_TITLE"
msgstr "Congratulations!"

