msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C0_S0_T0"
msgstr ""
"My loving Mother,\n"
"I write these Lines to acquaint you that I am at present in good Health, and hope they may find you in the same. I beg your Forgiveness for the manner of my leaving, for you deserved more than a hurried Farewell.\n"
"\n"
"I will not pretend I had no fear when I joined the Militia, but I find myself grinning at nothing as I write this, like a Fool. Father used to call me one and perhaps he was right.\n"
"\n"
"God willing I shall return to you, and when I do, you will want for nothing. I did not take up this Musket for nothing, and I do not wish to be any other way.\n"
"\n"
"The Sun is rising as I write, and I am now almost at Concord. I have had no sleep this night.\n"
"\n"
"Please to remember me to all enquiring Friends."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C0_S1_T0"
msgstr ""
"My dear Mother,\n"
"I have arrived in good Spirits. The ground is still soft from the Melt, and a Man's boots are never dry. We do not complain, and some even find a kind of humour in it.\n"
"\n"
"I have made the acquaintance of a Man named Abner. He meant to swim the river yesterday, though I told him the water was cold enough to stop a Man's Heart. He only laughed at me.\n"
"\n"
"But today we are all quiet. The Redcoats are close, but a Man who stands for something is twice the Man who does not. May we all stand and withstand what is to come.\n"
"\n"
"If God permits, I shall write againe soon."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C0_S2_T0"
msgstr ""
"We met the King's Men at the Bridge in Concord and the firing was nearer than I had ever known. I felt it... before I heard it. A Redcoat stood over me and what followed I cannot clearly remember... only that I am not the same as I was before it.\n"
"\n"
"I have had no sleep this night. The moaning did much disturb me... whether it was my own or that of the Men I could not well tell. I did not hear Abner's voice for he is gone. I regret now he did not swim.\n"
"\n"
"I am writing with my left hand as the right was struck by a Musket ball. I do not wish to trouble you with it, Mother... but I must rest...\n"
"\n"
"I hope this be not a dream though perhaps it were better if it were."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C0_S3_T0"
msgstr ""
"I have not received a letter from you since I left Home, and I fear my last may have given you some worry... I should very much like it if you could send me some sugar, for we have little of it here, and I may be kept in this Place some good while yet.\n"
"\n"
"There is a Proclamation offering Pardon to Men who would lay down their Arms in Boston. This makes me want to pick up mine, but yet here I am... not able to rise.\n"
"\n"
"I have not been altogether well... the nights are the worst, and I find little rest in sweating through them. The Men go on as they must, and I lie awake and listen.\n"
"\n"
"The Chirurgeon looks at me in a way I do not much like...\n"
"\n"
"I will write to you soon, and let us hope it shall not be from this Place."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C0_S4_T0"
msgstr ""
"My dear Mother,\n"
"I thank you for your letter. You write that all is well at home, and perhaps it is. But I know your Hand, and I know when it is telling me what I wish to hear rather than what is so.\n"
"\n"
"The thought of you alone aches my Heart. I came here to stand for what is ours, and yet I find I have come away with very little, and left you with less. Father once said he wished he were young again, only so he might give his Life for Freedom. I reckon his Wish deserved a better Son than the one who now writes to you.\n"
"\n"
"I do not know when I shall be fit to march, or whether marching is still a thing I am fit for. I do not know much of anything at present, which is a poor condition for a Man who once thought he knew a great deal."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C0_S5_T0"
msgstr ""
"Mother,\n"
"It has been long since I last wrote, forgive me, but I had little to say.\n"
"\n"
"I have no doubt you have heard the News and read of it. Someone brought the Newspapers into camp, and I had the Declaration before my own Eyes. In New York they have pulled down the Statue of George the Third and drawn it through the Streets, and the Men here were loud and glad when word reached us, and for a short while I shared their joy.\n"
"\n"
"I was glad of Liberty upon the page, yet I am not certain what Liberty may be for a Man such as I am now.\n"
"\n"
"I shall drink this Night with the Men, and let the celebration carry me where it may.\n"
"\n"
"The arm is much the same. I say no more of it."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C0_S6_T0"
msgstr ""
"My loving Mother,\n"
"I am on the Road to New Jersey as the British took New York. They say it is Cornwallis behind us, and that he moves fast.\n"
"\n"
"The days are long, the ground is hard, and I find I do not mind it so much as perhaps I should. There is something in the Road itself that suits me, though I feel every stone beneath my worn soles. Camp duties await me when we arrive, and light Work they tell me, until I am fit for more.\n"
"\n"
"The arm is the same. I mention it only so you do not wonder. I think of it on the road more than I ought perhaps, and less than you might fear. The Chirurgeon speaks carefully, which is a thing I have learned to distrust in Men of his profession.\n"
"\n"
"The road is there, and my feet are on it, and I find that for now it is enough."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C0_S7_T0"
msgstr ""
"My honoured Mother,\n"
"We have arrived at camp. I slept well on the road, but here my rest did not hold. The News of losses in New York reached us the same evening. Fort Washington is taken, and near three thousand of our Men with it. I did not know any of them, which I suppose is a Mercy.\n"
"\n"
"I lay awake longer than I ought. When I did sleep, I dreamed of the Bridge. The Redcoat stood above me, and I thought to raise my arm and take him by the throat, but just as I was about to do so, I woke.\n"
"\n"
"But I will tell you what happened in the morning. I stood with my thoughts, which are a poor sort of Company, when General Washington passed. He saw me, though I do not know why he should have, among so many. He put his Hand on my shoulder, smiling. It lasted no longer than a breath, but I have thought of little else since.\n"
"\n"
"I know that, after all, my arm may rise when Fate requires it."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C0_S8_T0"
msgstr ""
"Mother,\n"
"We crossed the Delaware on Christmas Night, in the dark, and struck the Hessians before they knew we were upon them. I say we, but I, of course, stayed at camp.\n"
"\n"
"My body remembers things my Mind had half forgotten, and I find I am not so diminished as I feared. Today I fired a Musket for the first time in near a year.\n"
"\n"
"And it was not just any Musket. I found it this morning, lying in camp as though it had been waiting. There was a particular mark upon it. I wish to believe it was Abner's, but perhaps I am only wishing. It is heavier than my own, and I think it always was.\n"
"\n"
"The drilling has paid what it cost, and yet I find I cannot settle. Will the Men have me on the March, past injury and all? Will my finger find the trigger when the moment truly comes?"

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C0_S9_T0"
msgstr ""
"My loving Mother,\n"
"I write to you now with better News than I have had for some time. The drilling has gone as well as could be hoped, and better than I expected.\n"
"\n"
"They have set me againe among the Men, and I am to march with them tomorrow. I have thought often of what I wrote to you before, and I find I am not so full of words now as once I was. A Man may speak easily of Liberty when he has not yet been made to pay for it. I know the price now and I believe I do not wish it to be any cheaper.\n"
"\n"
"When I come home to you, it shall not be with empty Hands. You shall be a free Woman, Mother, not in name only, but in truth. That is still my Wish, and I hold to it.\n"
"\n"
"PS The road lies before us, and I shall march until my Shoes are worn through again."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C0_SI_T0"
msgstr ""
"For more than a decade before the first shots were fired, tensions between Britain and the American colonies had been rising. After the costly French and Indian War (1754–1763), Parliament imposed new taxes, including the Stamp Act, the Townshend Acts, and the Sugar Act. For years before this, Britain had largely left the colonies to govern themselves under a policy known as salutary neglect. The shift toward tighter control met resistance.\n"
"\n"
"Boston became the center of that resistance. After colonists dumped British tea into the harbor in 1773, Parliament passed the Intolerable Acts, which closed the port and restricted self-governance. Resistance grew, leading to the formation of militias and the First Continental Congress.\n"
"\n"
"In 1775, fighting began at Lexington and Concord. The colonies united under George Washington, and the Declaration of Independence in 1776 turned rebellion into revolution. That same year, British forces drove Washington out of New York, but his victory at Trenton kept the revolution alive.\n"
"\n"
"Just before the Battles of Lexington and Concord, a young man named David Clark joined the militia. His letters trace his experience of the war."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C1_S0_T0"
msgstr ""
"To Major Edward Thompson, Province of Quebec, April 1778\n"
"I write in an unofficial capacity, having been relieved of duties these several days. I have a fever, and some trouble with the chest. Nothing of material Consequence, though the Regimental Surgeon has seen fit to be rather insistent on the Matter. I shall employ the interval to present a candid Account of recent events.\n"
"\n"
"General Burgoyne surrendered the whole of his Force on the 17th of October. However, there is a further Matter I find myself disinclined to commit to official Record. I shall write it here instead.\n"
"\n"
"Something occurred in the course of recent Operations and I have not yet determined what to make of it. You will apprehend my meaning when I have finished.\n"
"\n"
"I confess, Thompson, I find I cannot stop thinking of it."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C1_S1_T0"
msgstr ""
"Before I write the Account I promised, I find it necessary to establish certain Matters of Principle.\n"
"\n"
"I have served the Crown for many years. In that time I have witnessed the extent to which Order, maintained at considerable cost to the People of Britain, has secured the Prosperity of the Colonies. And it arrived not without our Treasury's expenditure. A Man does not call his Benefactor a Tyrant, or rather, a rational Man does not.\n"
"\n"
"I remain entirely sensible that this Rebellion is misguided, and that these Men fight in opposition to their own rational interests. I hold this view as firmly as I have held anything in my life.\n"
"\n"
"I must ask you to keep these pages private, as this is a Report from a British Officer... but also your Friend."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C1_S2_T0"
msgstr ""
"I shall now describe the events as I witnessed them.\n"
"\n"
"The position at Saratoga was defensible, and the Men were disciplined. I had not, at any point prior to the 17th of October, entertained the possibility that untrained Militia, a crowd of farmers and tradesmen bearing arms, could compel our Army to lay down its own. But I did that day.\n"
"\n"
"You will have received my official Account of the Surrender. What I could not mention there was Miss McCrea.\n"
"\n"
"I trust you have heard, as the news of her Death travelled so fast. A loyalist Woman, Thompson, killed by the very Warriors we employed... I will only note that it did considerable damage to our position among the neutral population. In the months since her Death in July, I have found it difficult to set it aside."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C1_S3_T0"
msgstr ""
"We were relocated to Philadelphia shortly after, and I found the change of scene unexpectedly welcome.\n"
"\n"
"In the days following Saratoga, the Men were quiet in a manner I had not previously observed. There was, however, a sentiment expressed with some regularity; this was one loss, and one loss only, and the rebellion remained entirely within our power to suppress. I believed this to be true.\n"
"\n"
"Philadelphia afforded a degree of Order and Steadiness that the field could not. A Man of Discipline should find his head most clear in the presence of Structure. It was also in Philadelphia that the events I have been circling in these Reports finally began."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C1_S4_T0"
msgstr ""
"I was to scout the surrounding territory with a small group of Men.\n"
"\n"
"We moved through woodland to the north of the city. The morning was clear and cold. The air was uncommonly fresh, and carried a particular stillness of the kind one does not encounter in a city, or perhaps does not think to notice. I found, as we ventured further from Philadelphia, that I was not entirely displeased to be alive. The first time I had felt that since Saratoga.\n"
"\n"
"As I write this in the black of night, I remember that day as the last day of my calm. I think now that a Man must have nothing unsettled in his mind to truly perceive such stillness and I have not done so since."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C1_S5_T0"
msgstr ""
"On the second day, we ventured even further from the city than before. The Men were weary, and moved out at dawn without a word between them.\n"
"\n"
"We were ambushed. A small party emerged from the tree line armed with rifles and knives. The Men, some of them Boys, in truth, were clearly unfit for any regular army. They were not, by any measure, Soldiers.\n"
"\n"
"We captured all of them. Some cuts and bruises among my Men, but nothing of Consequence. I had the rebels bound for transport back to Philadelphia, as they would be of use for interrogation. This, I noted in my official Account, was further Proof of the superiority of our Arms and Discipline over the rebels.\n"
"\n"
"I leave that sentence to stand, Thompson, in the event these pages should reach eyes other than yours."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C1_S6_T0"
msgstr ""
"I decided to oversee the interrogation personally.\n"
"\n"
"I will describe the prisoners to you as I found them. Several were boys, perhaps no more than sixteen or seventeen years of age. Others were older Men, well past any reasonable age of service. They were cold, hungry, and in poor condition generally.\n"
"\n"
"And yet, they looked at us directly, and without the deference one might reasonably expect from Men in their circumstances. There was something in their eyes that I could only describe, with some reluctance, as Fire. I found it, I will confess, somewhat unpleasant.\n"
"\n"
"I had been a Soldier for so long that I believed myself inured to finding anything unpleasant."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C1_S7_T0"
msgstr ""
"I made a decision which I ask you to receive without immediate Judgement. I elected to guard one of the prisoners personally for several nights. My stated reasoning was sound; a senior Officer present during the hours of darkness might draw out information that a junior would not.\n"
"\n"
"I will be candid with you, as I have endeavoured to be throughout this Report. There was another reason, which I found more difficult to name at the time, and which I will attempt to name now. I wished to examine that Fire more closely, as there was no rational cause for it. I tried to convince myself it was the Desperation of the cornered, mistaken for Conviction.\n"
"\n"
"The Man was thin and visibly unwell, not from any wound sustained, but from some illness he had brought with him out of Valley Forge. I offered him a meal. He refused it."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C1_S8_T0"
msgstr ""
"The Man was not disposed toward cooperation. Yet in time he accepted the meal. We spoke over the course of three nights before, as it transpired, that meal proved to be his last.\n"
"\n"
"I had expected and prepared for the usual catalogue of colonial Grievances. What I was not prepared for was the Man himself. For the Zeal in this Man's eyes as he was dying, feverish and coughing.\n"
"\n"
"I was inclined to persuade myself that such Conviction in a dying Man might be the untethered mind, but I knew it was not. It occurred to me, for the first time, that these were not merely rebellious subjects to be suppressed, but the resolve of a People at War, whose Cause might prove far more formidable than any rebellion in a Colony. The Thought arrived. I found I could not dismiss it.\n"
"\n"
"I am not certain that the Man who is writing these words now is entirely the same Man who set out from Philadelphia."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C1_S9_T0"
msgstr ""
"The prisoner died with a smile on his face. I believe he knew that he managed to gain a victory before departing. Not with a rifle or knife, but with three nights of speaking to a hated enemy who had come to interrogate him.\n"
"\n"
"When I look in the glass, I see Ardour in my own eyes. I do not know whether that is on account of the fever and tightness in the chest. Perhaps that Man changed something in me quite literally after all. The Surgeon remains insistent that I stay isolated and in bed.\n"
"\n"
"I am certain this document has become more of a Confession than a Report...War may do that to a Man, it appears, given Cause enough.\n"
"\n"
"I should like to know what you make of all this. I ask that you write to me presently, while there is yet time.\n"
"\n"
"Your Friend,\n"
"Major J. Harris"

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C1_SI_T0"
msgstr ""
"By 1777, the revolution had survived but remained fragile, as the Continental Army was undersupplied and undertrained.\n"
"\n"
"Britain's strategy was to send three armies to meet near Albany, cut New England off from the south, and suppress the resistance. The plan fell apart, however, when General Howe, the British Commander-in-Chief, decided to march on Philadelphia, the seat of the Continental Congress, believing its capture would demoralize the colonists and end the rebellion quickly. He took the city, but left General Burgoyne to advance south from Canada alone, without the reinforcements the plan required. Outnumbered by growing American forces, Burgoyne surrendered his entire army at Saratoga in October 1777.\n"
"\n"
"With Philadelphia now under British control, Washington led the men to winter quarters at Valley Forge. The soldiers faced rough conditions, as they lacked supplies, and disease spread through the camp. Yet Valley Forge was also where the army was rebuilt due to constant training and drilling.\n"
"\n"
"In the aftermath of these events, British Major Jonathan Harris, who had believed from the start that order would prevail, recounts his experiences in an unofficial report."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C2_S0_T0"
msgstr ""
"A mariner must have great stories! It is the whole point of going anywhere at all.\n"
"\n"
"I have told many in my time. Of storms, mysterious islands, cunning enemies, and all the things that befall a young Frenchman who cannot remain in one place. And I confess, some of my stories—they have some exaggerations. A story is a living creature, so it is only natural it should change with time.\n"
"\n"
"But this one, this one is of a different nature. This is my last story told, and I wish to preserve it.\n"
"\n"
"I have been long enough at sea to know that most men return from it with great noise and very little else, speaking at length of what they endured, and in twenty years there is nobody who can recall a single word of it.\n"
"\n"
"I did not desire that fate. I wished to remain in history. To have men speak my name as they speak the names of the great ones.\n"
"\n"
"And so, when the american Revolution was commencing and France was, enfin, resolved to act, I saw my moment as plainly as I have ever seen anything."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C2_S1_T0"
msgstr ""
"I was in a tavern, drinking with some fellow mariniers—bien sûr, this is what a man did while between ships and decisions, and I was both. There was a man on the other side of the room. His stare was bidding us stop our shouting.\n"
"\n"
"And then he spoke. He promised to hunt english ships and a battle worth the telling, but once he said the word \"privateer\", it was enough.\n"
"\n"
"The drunken men around me answered the call and had likely forgot what they had done by the next morning, but I had only finished my first drink. The voice in my head said, \"André, this is your chance for a great adventure. You are a daring mariner, are you not?\"\n"
"\n"
"I found my way to the crew, and the adventure I thought would bring me fame and glory began."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C2_S2_T0"
msgstr ""
"The ship was the Bonhomme Richard, or as I preferred to call him, Duc de Duras.\n"
"\n"
"The crew were seasoned mariniers, men who had no ropes left to learn and who found it hard walking upon a steady floor. We sailed for nearly ten days and, oh, did we bring havoc wherever we went!\n"
"\n"
"The battle off the coast of Yorkshire was incroyable! At some point, two ships, ours and the Serapis, were lashed together, hull to hull, facing in opposite directions—which sounds very much like a mistake, but there we were.\n"
"\n"
"Their guns were touching our hull planks, and we were so close I believe I could make out the faces of the men trying to kill me. There was a fire on our own deck. Smoke so thick you could lose a man standing right beside you. And then—I swear it is true!—one of our own ships, the Alliance, sailed past and fired upon us. I do not know to this day whether it was an accident.\n"
"\n"
"In that battle, I felt like the happiest man alive! I had always known I was born for the sea, but I wanted even more.\n"
"\n"
"I joined the french navy—not for routine duties, bah—but because a man who had seen what I had seen deserved a rank. Admiral, perhaps... and then, in 1780, I was sent to les Antilles."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C2_S3_T0"
msgstr ""
"The voyage was long and épuisant. The provisions spoiled within the first week. The rats—oh, they did not care what they ate, nor how it tasted, nor anything for that matter. The ship never ceased moving, never ceased making noise, and the rats, it seems, followed the cue.\n"
"\n"
"One day upon the deck, the ocean was so calm that I stood persuaded there was no movement in it at all. It seemed to me that we were set within an endless expanse of water and sky, both of the same grey. One stood ready to swallow us, the other to unleash the wrath of God.\n"
"\n"
"Battles are easier for me. The vastness and the stillness make me afraid of death. There I stood, praying only that the storm which always follows the calm would not devour me, and might yet permit me to see one more battle and one more femme."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C2_S4_T0"
msgstr ""
"The following night I was woken by the storm. I knew it would come, and the mariniers were prepared for it. But in truth, you are never entirely prepared.\n"
"\n"
"I went up on deck and the lightning was illuminating the horizon every few seconds. Oh mon dieu, the fury of the ocean is something mesmerising that draws you in, as though the sirens are somewhere bidding you follow them. And only your fellow men shouting profanities whilst trying to save the ship can pull you out of the trance.\n"
"\n"
"A strike of lightning betrayed a british ship of the line upon the horizon. The silhouette was there for a second and then gone, like a sea beast.\n"
"\n"
"We went unnoticed. As they passed, every man let out a long breath. The next morning was calm, and the tafia tasted as it never had before. We were nearing our destination."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C2_S5_T0"
msgstr ""
"We arrived in the Caraïbes, and I will tell you, after that voyage, arriving somewhere warm and green and full of people who had not been at sea for months felt like a gift from God himself.\n"
"\n"
"We went to explore the island—and of course, within the hour, I was in a tavern with the crew.\n"
"\n"
"I was in fine spirits, as one could tell, for I was telling stories. Parts of this very one, in fact—the only difference being that the english had spotted us and we had been obliged to fight them in the storm. My own composure during it had grown considerably in the retelling. The men were laughing.\n"
"\n"
"I observed that a young, dark-haired woman at the next table was listening and not laughing, which I took, at the time, as a sign of admiration. When I had finished, she looked at me and said, \"With that attitude, I should not be surprised if you lost your life soon. Try to keep that head upon your shoulders.\" \"And what would you know of it?\" I replied. \"You have never sailed anywhere.\"\n"
"\n"
"We ordered more guildive, and that was the end of it.\n"
"\n"
"Alone, the silhouette in the lightning returned to me, and I remembered my hands upon the railing, gripping hard. I did not tell her that part, nor my foolish thoughts of sirens."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C2_S6_T0"
msgstr ""
"I had forgotten that woman in a stuffy tavern as my voyages continued, but her words returned to me when our fleet arrived in Chesapeake Bay at the call of George Washington, as I read later.\n"
"\n"
"As we lay in the bay, the flagship of Admiral de Grasse was wanting nearly two hundred men, more than a thousand of our mariniers being up the estuary with the transports. We were to fight short-handed, the wind against us, on a lee shore.\n"
"\n"
"I found myself gripping the railings even harder than I had in the storm near the Antilles, as I overheard my officer say to another—not intended for my ears—that he was not certain we would make it out alive, let alone defeat the english. \"I should not be surprised if you lost your life soon.\" The words beat in my head.\n"
"\n"
"The night before the battle I wrote a letter to my mother and placed it in my coat. I told her the Caraïbes were beautiful."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C2_S7_T0"
msgstr ""
"The english came through the channel and formed a perfect line.\n"
"\n"
"What I understood, standing upon that deck, was that the battle ahead was not to be won by courage, but by patience and positioning. Not once in my life had I given much thought to either.\n"
"\n"
"We manoeuvred for hours, feeling for the advantage; by the time the battle began in earnest, it was past four in the afternoon and the sun was already dropping.\n"
"\n"
"Our guns were aimed high, directly at their rigging. A ship with shredded sails and a fallen mast cannot escape you."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C2_S8_T0"
msgstr ""
"Our Ensign was killed within minutes of the battle commencing. I took his place—I cannot say precisely why; I was the closest one, I suppose.\n"
"\n"
"Through the thick smoke I could see pieces of timber tearing through the men as the ships discharged their guns. I could hear screams and groans, and I had to shout and command above them. I had to be louder and faster. These were the longest hours of my life, and somehow also the shortest.\n"
"\n"
"The voice in my head—the one that had once sent me to trouble the english in the North Sea—that day bade me only to keep the men moving, keep them alive.\n"
"\n"
"We assisted our allies in defeating the feared enemy, but I did not think upon those things. I only counted how many men lived, and how many fell."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C2_S9_T0"
msgstr ""
"I shall never be remembered for the Chesapeake. No history will carry my name—only this short account I have written for you, mon ami, before I go out into the streets of Paris to fight for our own Liberté, so that you may read it should I not return.\n"
"\n"
"I believe I did alter the course of that war, as did Admiral de Grasse and Washington, and every man who lived or perished in that bay. The only difference is that our names will not be spoken of.\n"
"\n"
"We had bled in that fight for freedom across the ocean. I had not considered, then, how much it would cost, and I can only imagine how much we shall pay for our own. I see the heads of men I have known fall, and I cannot number them, nor preserve them, as I could not preserve all the men on that ship.\n"
"\n"
"I am certain there will be no want of stories about our revolution, but I am no longer a teller of stories.\n"
"\n"
"À Henry, de ton ami André Bernard,\n"
"Paris, 1790"

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C2_SI_T0"
msgstr ""
"Following the American victory at Saratoga in 1777, France formally entered the war on the American side, signing the Treaty of Alliance in February 1778. Britain, which had until then been fighting a colonial rebellion, was now engaged in a global conflict. With French fleets challenging British control of the Atlantic, Britain was forced to divert significant resources away from North America to Europe and the Caribbean. The Royal Navy faced coordinated pressure on multiple fronts as Spain declared its alliance with France in 1779.\n"
"\n"
"In September 1779, the war at sea reached British home waters when an American-led squadron operating from France engaged an escort frigate off the Yorkshire coast near Flamborough Head.\n"
"\n"
"In September of 1781, the French fleet under Admiral de Grasse sailed north from the Caribbean and engaged a British naval force at the mouth of Chesapeake Bay, making the final campaign of the war possible.\n"
"\n"
"A young French sailor, André Bernard, joined the navy in search of adventure, with little regard for politics. His story follows the major battles and what they took from him."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C3_S0_T0"
msgstr ""
"An Artist is but a Vessel.\n"
"\n"
"I have always believed it so, ever since my Master told me one evening. The difficulty lies in that I have felt myself an empty Vessel all my life. I waited for something to fill me and to speak through me. I sought it in People, in Places, and in every Canvas set before me, yet found only absence. I never expected what would come to guide my hand. Somewhere between the first brushstroke I ever made and those I now lay upon the Canvas, a War was fought.\n"
"\n"
"I was present for it. It is with this that I shall begin my Memoir."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C3_S1_T0"
msgstr ""
"I am young, though old enough to be obliged to sustain myself alone. I visit my Master when I am able, which is not so often as I would wish, for the Matters of the Heart are of great importance to a young Man. Many evenings in pleasant company have left me less than clear-headed the next morning. Perhaps I am a Vessel of Love and Beauty?\n"
"\n"
"My Master tells me I have great Talent. I wish I could believe him with the whole of my Conviction, but Divine Providence, it seems, takes little interest in a Man's Vocation. I cannot devote my Soul to the Craft as I would like. I would starve if I did.\n"
"\n"
"I have sought a Patron with persistence but to no avail. Yet I cannot persuade myself that I am mistaken in what I am meant to do.\n"
"\n"
"One morning, a Letter arrived that changed everything."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C3_S2_T0"
msgstr ""
"The Letter arrived on a Wednesday. I remember that distinctly, because Wednesdays are when I paint signs for our tradesmen and this pays for the rest of the week. I was in no humour to be interrupted.\n"
"\n"
"It was an Invitation to dinner. Brief and written in a hand that implied both great Wealth and considerable Influence. I read it twice before I looked at the signature. And once I did, I read it a third time.\n"
"\n"
"The name belonged to a Merchant from Williamsburg of some considerable standing. A Man, in short, who did not typically concern himself with Painters of no particular reputation who owed three months of rent. Why me?\n"
"\n"
"I usually distrust good Fortune, but I resolved to go."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C3_S3_T0"
msgstr ""
"At the dinner, I learned it was my Master who had recommended me. He had spoken well of my Abilities... better, I suspect, than I deserve, and the Merchant had taken him at his word. The offer itself was simple enough. I was to paint the War, and in return he would see to my expenses. He wished to own at least one great Work depicting a fearsome battle against the British Army, something worthy of Posterity.\n"
"\n"
"It was everything I had wanted. The freedom to paint in earnest, without the Wednesday Signs. And yet something in me resisted... when the moment I had longed for arrived, I found myself afraid to meet it.\n"
"\n"
"Will I be able to let Providence speak through me upon the Canvas?"

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C3_S4_T0"
msgstr ""
"Despite my apprehension, I accepted. I knew it to be my only chance, and I am not so vain as to deny it.\n"
"\n"
"I gathered what accounts I could of the past battles, spoke with Men who had been present, and read what had been written of the Conflict. Then I stood before the Canvas and began.\n"
"\n"
"I imagined Lexington and Concord. I strove to feel what those Militia Men must have felt. I pictured the summer of 1776 in Philadelphia, the heat, and the weight of the Decision those Men undertook. My thoughts turned to Saratoga, to Valley Forge, to those who endured the cold with quiet Resolve.\n"
"\n"
"I looked upon the Canvases, seeking to discern what it was they lacked. I knew they were not what I envisioned them to be and the difficulty lay not in my Hand."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C3_S5_T0"
msgstr ""
"It may not have been apparent to another eye. When my Paintings were later praised, I received such words with what Grace I could.\n"
"\n"
"But I knew. I was bringing the most essential parts from Imagination. The particular quality of the light at Saratoga, the precise look a Man bears in his Eye when he first sees a Redcoat. And a painting founded upon invention, however skilful, is a kind of Falsehood. What had I truly seen, but tavern signs, Nature, and ladies?\n"
"\n"
"It occurred to me that I might have to join the Army. I entertained the notion for the better part of an afternoon. I am not, by constitution, a Soldier.\n"
"\n"
"In that moment of contemplation, I did not know how near my greatest Work already was, nor how soon I should be called upon to paint it."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C3_S6_T0"
msgstr ""
"Word reached me that they marched toward our Town. The glorious Washington and the French Troops, converging near Williamsburg. Was this Providence?\n"
"\n"
"And then they were here, right at my doorstep. The Man who led us toward Freedom, and his Allies.\n"
"\n"
"People were closing the doors in panic, but I took my easel and stepped out. The streets were emptying around me, the People fled in droves, but I walked in the opposite direction, and some part of me observed this with perfect Calm, as though it were happening to someone else.\n"
"\n"
"And then, I thought, \"If this is God taking your hand, who are you not to follow?\""

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C3_S7_T0"
msgstr ""
"It was Chaos. Cannon and Musket fire, Men running and falling, smoke so thick it seemed the Sun had already set, though it was yet morning.\n"
"\n"
"The allied guns were firing from three directions at once. They had taken the British Redoubts in the night, I learned afterward, Hamilton's men storming No. 10 with Bayonets and not a Musket loaded, hacking through the abatis in the dark.\n"
"\n"
"I found a place upon an elevation where the view opened, and set myself to depict the scene.\n"
"\n"
"My brush moved... and then it all went blank."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C3_S8_T0"
msgstr ""
"The next thing I remember I was standing before the Canvas in the Atelier, and the Painting was finished.\n"
"\n"
"It was complete in a Manner I had never achieved before. I think it was not my hand alone that painted the red earth, the Men upon it, and the soaring Flag of our Liberty.\n"
"\n"
"My Master said an artist is merely a Vessel. I had written that down at the beginning of this and always believed it, deep within me. I was only ever afraid that nothing would wish to speak through me.\n"
"\n"
"The precise quality of light I had sought when I carried Saratoga in my mind and never found, the look in a Man's eye that I had tried to imagine and always fallen short of. All of it was there upon the Canvas. I believe all the Men who fought for Freedom spoke through me near Yorktown that day at the Victory's Dawn."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C3_S9_T0"
msgstr ""
"The Merchant was more than satisfied. He covered my expenses in full and offered me further Work. He spoke of the painting with a feeling that I believe was genuine.\n"
"\n"
"I was not certain what I expected to feel. The satisfaction of a Man who has at last done the thing he was longing for?\n"
"\n"
"What I felt was closer to emptiness. The War was ending and the Nation that had paid such Cost for its Freedom was beginning. And I was standing in the Atelier with clean brushes and no particular notion of what comes next. My Master told me this is how it always is, after a great Work. That the Emptiness is not an ending but a Threshold.\n"
"\n"
"I had ended one chapter, and I knew not what the next would look like. As I now know, I stood on the verge of a great Journey. After years of struggle, I was free. But that I shall tell in the pages that follow."

msgid "IDS_NARRATIVE_CAMPAIGN_NARRATIVE_TEXT_BLOCK_C3_SI_T0"
msgstr ""
"By the autumn of 1781, Britain still held major coastal cities but had failed to defeat the Continental Army. In the south, General Cornwallis had fought a costly campaign through the Carolinas before moving his army north to Virginia, where he was to establish a fortified position at Yorktown and await resupply by sea. With the Chesapeake Bay now under French control, the resupply never came.\n"
"\n"
"Washington and Rochambeau marched their combined forces south in the longest troop movement of the entire war, and Cornwallis found himself surrounded.\n"
"\n"
"The siege of Yorktown began on September 28 and lasted three weeks, during which Franco-American artillery attacked the British defenses. On October 19, 1781, Cornwallis surrendered the army. It was the largest British capitulation of the war and the last major land campaign of the conflict.\n"
"\n"
"Among those who witnessed the final chapter of the struggle was an American painter, Edward Mercer, who recorded the revolution on canvas."

