msgid "IDS_PCZC1076_FROM_THE_BOTTOM_P2_1_1"
msgstr "Fritz X Strike on Roma, September 9, 1943"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1076_FROM_THE_BOTTOM_P2_1_1_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"The Fritz X was the world's first operational, radio-guided, anti-ship glide bomb. Developed in Germany during World War II to destroy heavily armored warships, it weighed about 1.6 tons. Designed by Max Kramer and built by Ruhrstahl, it was essentially a 1,600-kilogram armor-piercing bomb fitted with small wings and radio-controlled tail spoilers.\n"
"\n"
"An operator in the launching bomber released the Fritz X and guided it visually, making minor corrections to its range and bearing. Requiring a clear line of sight to the target, the pilot had to keep the aircraft steady and within view of the impact point, leaving the aircraft vulnerable to anti-aircraft fire and enemy fighters.\n"
"\n"
"The first successful combat use of the Fritz X took place on September 9, 1943, when two bombs sank the Italian battleship Roma. Soon afterward, Fritz X attacks struck and damaged several Allied ships, including U.S. light cruiser Savannah, Royal Navy cruiser Uganda, and battleship Warspite."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1077_FROM_THE_BOTTOM_P2_1_2"
msgstr "381/50 Ansaldo Mod. 1934 Naval Gun"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1077_FROM_THE_BOTTOM_P2_1_2_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"The 381/50 Ansaldo Mod. 1934 naval gun was designed for the Regia Marina in the 1930s and mounted on Italy's newest Littorio-class battleships. It was manufactured by Ansaldo and OTO and mounted in three triple-gun turrets with a maximum elevation of 35°. Three types of shells were developed for this gun: armor-piercing, semi-armor-piercing, and high-explosive. The latter was never used, however, as its nose fuse was considered too sensitive.\n"
"\n"
"Interestingly, battleship Roma was armed with guns manufactured by both Ansaldo and OTO. One turret housed Ansaldo guns and the other two—OTO-made ones. In total, the two companies produced 40 guns for the four Littorio-class battleships. The fourth ship of the class, Impero, was never completed and therefore never received her guns."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1078_FROM_THE_BOTTOM_P2_1_3"
msgstr "Italian 90/50 Mod. 1939 Heavy Anti-Aircraft Gun"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1078_FROM_THE_BOTTOM_P2_1_3_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"Shortly before the Second World War, Italian manufacturer Ansaldo developed a 90 mm/50-caliber anti-aircraft gun for the Regia Marina. After trials aboard armored cruiser San Giorgio, it was installed in shielded single mounts on the newest Littorio-class battleships and modernized Andrea Doria-class battleships. The guns were produced by Ansaldo from 1938 and under license by OTO starting in 1939.\n"
"\n"
"The mountings were quadriaxially stabilized and considered highly sophisticated for their time, offering good ballistic performance; however, they proved delicate at sea and were vulnerable to water damage. Although the gun was accurate, early high-explosive rounds tended to fragment into very fine splinters that were not always able to inflict decisive damage to aircraft.\n"
"\n"
"In addition to the naval gun, Ansaldo also developed the 90/53 Mod. 1939 version for the Italian Army."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1079_FROM_THE_BOTTOM_P2_1_4"
msgstr "Luftwaffe Kampfgeschwader 100 Emblem"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1079_FROM_THE_BOTTOM_P2_1_4_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"Throughout its service, the Kampfgeschwader 100 air unit took part in anti-shipping and minelaying operations across multiple theaters: the Black Sea, the Sea of Azov, the Volga River, and the Caspian Sea, as well as the Mediterranean, North Africa, and, later in the war, Southern France and the Western Front.\n"
"\n"
"During World War II, three subunits, or \"Gruppe\" in German, were formed within KG 100, each designated with Roman numerals: I., II., and III.\n"
"\n"
"In 1943, KG 100 became the first military aviation unit to use a precision-guided bomb in combat. The first attack with the Fritz X was carried out on July 21, 1943, at Augusta harbor. However, the unit's first major success came on September 9, when 11 Dornier Do 217 K-2 bombers from III./KG 100 attacked and sank Italian battleship Roma and damaged her sister ship Italia (formerly Littorio)."

msgid "IDS_PCZC107_NY2018_TORPEDOTUBE533MK8"
msgstr "Torpedo Tube of the British Destroyer"

msgid "IDS_PCZC107_NY2018_TORPEDOTUBE533MK8_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"During the Battle of the North Cape, the Allied ships launched 55 torpedoes, 47 of them were launched by seven destroyers. All those destroyers were built in England and armed with 533 mm caliber quadruple torpedo tubes. HMS Musketeer and HMS Matchless were carrying the Mk.X tubes. Other ships, that were ordered as part of the War Emergency Programme, were equipped with slightly different Mk.VIII tubes.\n"
"\n"
"The primary munition of the tubes were the 533 mm Mk.IX torpedoes which contained 367 kg of explosives and could hit a target at a distance of 10 km and at a speed of 41 knots. At least 11 torpedoes of this kind hit Scharnhorst in her final battle."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1084_BOTTOM_YAMATO_1_1"
msgstr "U.S. Aircraft Launch the Attack"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1084_BOTTOM_YAMATO_1_1_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"The destruction of Yamato was of paramount importance, as the battleship's firepower could pose a serious threat to the landing troops. Thus, American forces could not allow the Japanese task force to reach Okinawa. The forces concentrated by the U.S. Navy command for the attack were capable of destroying an entire fleet. Against one battleship, a cruiser, and eight destroyers, the U.S. Navy assembled eleven aircraft carriers, six of the newest South Dakota and Iowa-class battleships, eleven cruisers, and several dozen destroyers.\n"
"\n"
"On the morning of April 7, U.S. reconnaissance aircraft spotted the Japanese fleet. One by one, Grumman F6F Hellcat and Vought F4U Corsair fighters, Curtiss SB2C Helldiver dive bombers, and Grumman TBF Avenger torpedo bombers began taking off from carrier decks. Nearly 400 aircraft surged toward the Japanese formation."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1085_BOTTOM_YAMATO_1_2"
msgstr "Japanese 2nd Fleet Heading for the Final Battle"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1085_BOTTOM_YAMATO_1_2_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"The Japanese 2nd Fleet, led by Yamato, embarked on its final sortie on April 6, 1945. The night passed quietly, and at the dawn of April 7, the fleet's commander, Vice Admiral Itō, ordered the ships to form a defensive formation to repel the aircraft attack. Yamato took position at the center of the formation, while the destroyers encircled her, each spaced roughly 1.5 km apart. Cruiser Yahagi led the formation, advancing at a speed of 20 knots.\n"
"\n"
"The fleet's first loss was destroyer Asashimo. The ship experienced engine trouble, began to fall behind, and soon turned back. The rest of the task force continued on its course toward the enemy landing troops. It was in this formation that the 2nd Fleet was spotted by American reconnaissance aircraft shortly before it entered its final battle."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1086_BOTTOM_YAMATO_1_3"
msgstr "Carrier-Borne Aircraft Attack"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1086_BOTTOM_YAMATO_1_3_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"The 2nd Fleet had no air cover, and the few available Japanese planes from nearby airfields could not counter the American aircraft, which dominated the skies without opposition. AA fire from the Japanese ships also failed to disrupt the concentrated aerial assault.\n"
"\n"
"The American pilots launched a powerful torpedo and bomb strike against the formation. Aircraft that had already dropped their payloads continued to strafe the ships with gunfire, aiming to overwhelm the enemy's air defenses through sheer numbers. During the first wave alone, Yamato was hit by both bombs and torpedoes, while Yahagi was immobilized by a successful strike to the engine room. Forty minutes after the first wave, the second one followed, and half an hour after that came the third, which put an end to any lingering hopes the Japanese might have had to turn the tide of war."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1087_BOTTOM_YAMATO_1_4"
msgstr "Yamato's Final Moments"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1087_BOTTOM_YAMATO_1_4_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"The Japanese sailors knew they were heading to their deaths, yet they were prepared to fulfill their duty to the very end. On April 7, 1945, at 14:20, Yamato capsized after being struck by 20 torpedoes and aerial bombs. Three minutes later, a massive explosion erupted through the ship as the main battery magazines detonated. It was so powerful that it tore the ship in two, sending a mushroom-shaped cloud more than 6 km into the sky. The explosion, visible and audible from over 200 km away, stands as one of the largest non-nuclear man-made explosions in human history.\n"
"\n"
"Vice Admiral Seiichi Itō, commander of the task force, and Kōsaku Aruga, Yamato's captain, refused to abandon the ship. Of the battleship's crew of over 3,000, fewer than 300 sailors and officers survived."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1088_AZURLANE_8_1_1"
msgstr "Who Doesn't Love Cat(girl)s?"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1088_AZURLANE_8_1_1_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
