msgid "IDS_PCZC1188_HUNT_BISMARCK_1_1"
msgstr "German Raiders on the Way to the North Atlantic"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1188_HUNT_BISMARCK_1_1_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"In the early hours of May 21, 1941, German battleship Bismarck and heavy cruiser Prinz Eugen slipped out of Grimstadfjord near Bergen, Norway, to begin Operation Rheinübung. Their mission was to break into the North Atlantic and attack Allied merchant shipping.\n"
"\n"
"Maintaining strict radio silence and screened by low clouds, rain, and mist, the ships moved through the fjords and then north along the Norwegian coast. As they steered toward the Denmark Strait, they encountered worsening weather, fog, and drifting ice, which reduced visibility while they pressed on toward their fateful encounter with HMS Hood."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1189_HUNT_BISMARCK_1_2"
msgstr "Battle of the Denmark Strait"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1189_HUNT_BISMARCK_1_2_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"At dawn on May 24, 1941, in the Denmark Strait, Royal Navy capital ships HMS Hood and Prince of Wales engaged their German counterparts. Closing in at high speed, Hood opened fire first, but the British initially targeted the wrong ship, concentrating fire on Prinz Eugen. As the range closed, both German ships focused on Hood.\n"
"\n"
"Minutes into the action, a salvo from Bismarck struck close to Hood's aft ammunition magazines. A vast explosion split the battlecruiser in two. She sank within minutes, leaving only three survivors from a crew of more than 1,400. Bismarck, damaged but still operational, turned south into the Atlantic."

msgid "IDS_PCZC118_NY2018_Z29_SHIP"
msgstr "Destroyer Z-29"

msgid "IDS_PCZC118_NY2018_Z29_SHIP_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"Besides Scharnhorst, the Kriegsmarine Force, which was operating in the Arctic waters in December 1943, included the 4th Destroyer Flotilla comprising five ships. Two of them—Z-29 and Z-30—were of the 1936A class; the remaining three—Z-33, Z-34, and Z-38—were classed as 1936A (Mob). All these ships were armed with four or five 149 mm guns and two quadruple 533 mm torpedo tubes. Their maximum speed could reach 37 knots.\n"
"\n"
"While searching for the Allied convoy on the morning of December 26, 1943, due to stormy weather and the arctic night conditions, the destroyers lost sight of their flagship just before the first engagement with the British ships. After a few hours, the commander of the German naval force, Konteradmiral Bey, ordered them to return to the base. In the battle that followed, Scharnhorst was left alone and sunk. Destroyers, which could seriously disrupt the actions of the British forces and provide a chance for the battleship to escape the trap, safely returned to the Norwegian fjords without even facing the enemy."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1190_HUNT_BISMARCK_1_3"
msgstr "Hunters and Prey"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1190_HUNT_BISMARCK_1_3_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"On the evening of May 26, 1941, aircraft carrier HMS Ark Royal launched Swordfish torpedo bombers against Bismarck, which was making her way toward Brest through stormy seas. Earlier attacks had failed or mistakenly targeted HMS Sheffield, but a final wave of Swordfish pressed home through cloud and heavy anti-aircraft fire. \n"
"\n"
"Approaching at low level, they dropped torpedoes from different bearings, forcing Bismarck into violent turns. One torpedo struck near the stern, jamming her rudders hard to port. Crippled and unable to steer, Bismarck began circling in the open sea. This single hit sealed her fate, preventing Bismarck from getting into the area where the Luftwaffe could cover her retreat to France."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1191_HUNT_BISMARCK_1_4"
msgstr "Bismarck's Final Battle"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1191_HUNT_BISMARCK_1_4_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"At dawn on May 27, 1941, a crippled Bismarck faced British naval forces, including battleships HMS King George V and HMS Rodney, on heavy seas. Unable to maneuver after the fateful torpedo hit, she was quickly surrounded and pounded with heavy shells at close range.\n"
"\n"
"Bismarck's final engagement lasted less than an hour. Her main turrets were smashed, the superstructure wrecked, and fires raged along the decks as cruisers and destroyers joined the attack. With Bismarck burning, British ships closed in to finish her with gunfire and torpedoes. By mid-morning, Bismarck had finally sunk, with only 116 crew members surviving out of a crew of more than 2,200."

