msgid "IDS_PCZC1372_NAVAL_LEGACY_CHIKUMA_1_1"
msgstr "Destroyer Escort Samuel B. Roberts Hull Number"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1372_NAVAL_LEGACY_CHIKUMA_1_1_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"Destroyer escort Samuel B. Roberts (DE-413) played a vital role "
"in countering Chikuma off Samar. As the cruiser opened fire on "
"the vulnerable American carriers, Lieutenant Commander Robert W. Copeland "
"pushed his engines to the limit, closed to point-blank range, and engaged "
"the giant. He famously declared to his crew, \"This will be a fight against "
"overwhelming odds from which survival cannot be expected. We will do what "
"damage we can.\" Through masterful maneuvering, the tiny destroyer endured "
"an uneven duel, mauling the cruiser's superstructure before air attacks "
"ultimately severed Chikuma's stern. At 09:00, shells from battleship Kongō "
"knocked out Samuel B. Roberts' power, forcing her crew to abandon ship "
"before she finally sank at 10:05. For this extraordinary heroism, "
"the destroyer shared the Presidential Unit Citation awarded to her task "
"unit."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1373_NAVAL_LEGACY_CHIKUMA_1_2"
msgstr "Main Battery Turrets After a Duel With a U.S. Destroyer"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1373_NAVAL_LEGACY_CHIKUMA_1_2_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"A distinctive feature of Chikuma was her forward-concentrated main battery of 203 mm twin-gun mounts, which freed the aft deck for floatplane operations. Grouping all four turrets forward also allowed a highly concentrated and efficient armor scheme for their magazines. However, to avoid severe top-weight issues, turret armor had to be compromised, leaving the turrets protected by a mere 25 mm of steel plates.\n"
"\n"
"This vulnerability became apparent during her duel with American escorts, where Chikuma's turrets suffered severe damage. The destroyer escort Samuel B. Roberts unleashed a hail of 127 mm shells at short range, expending nearly her entire ammunition. Gun Captain Paul H. Carr distinguished himself the most, firing about 300 shots within 35 minutes until his gun suffered a breech explosion, mortally wounding him."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1374_NAVAL_LEGACY_CHIKUMA_1_3"
msgstr "U.S. TBM Avenger Dropping a Torpedo"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1374_NAVAL_LEGACY_CHIKUMA_1_3_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"The arrival of the Avenger torpedo bombers turned the tide of the battle. "
"To ensure the success of the attack, they employed the deadly \"hammer and "
"anvil\" tactic, splitting into groups to attack Chikuma from both sides. "
"Intense gunfire from the American destroyers, though unable to penetrate the"
" main armor belt, heavily damaged the superstructure and wiped out most "
"of the anti-aircraft crew, clearing the way for the bombers. However, "
"despite these catastrophic losses, the gunners remaining on Chikuma fought "
"fiercely and continued firing amid the carnage, refusing to surrender until "
"the very end."

msgid "IDS_PCZC1375_NAVAL_LEGACY_CHIKUMA_1_4"
msgstr "Chikuma's Hull After a Torpedo Hit"

msgid "IDS_PCZC1375_NAVAL_LEGACY_CHIKUMA_1_4_DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""
"Chikuma's fate was sealed at 08:53 when a torpedo struck her aft port side. Eyewitnesses recalled: \"There was a burst of flame and simultaneously a column of water almost as high as the length of the ship shot up in the air. The after deck single mount machine gun and other gear were seen blown in the air. The after half of the after deck was apparently heavily damaged and settled in the water but ship continued to move at slow speed and her guns kept on firing.\" Her captain radioed: \"One propeller, speed 18 knots, unable to steer.\"\n"
"\n"
"At 11:05, a new attack scored hits under the bridge and mainmast. With heavy smoke billowing, the cruiser lost headway and listed to port. At 14:30, after three final torpedoes ripped open the boiler room, Chikuma capsized and sank."

